  "worked_solution": "Applying the root and quotient rules, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 2}\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{x}^{2}-4x+5}} & =\\sqrt[3]{{lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{x}^{2}-4x+5}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{{lim}_{x\\to 2}({x}^{3})}{{lim}_{x\\to 2}({x}^{2}-4x+5)}}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the limits of the numerator and denominator separately. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 2}({x}^{3}) & ={2}^{3} \\\\  & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 2}({x}^{2}-4x+5) & ={2}^{2}-4(2)+5 \\\\  & =4-8+5 \\\\  & =1\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{{lim}_{x\\to 2}({x}^{3})}{{lim}_{x\\to 2}({x}^{2}-4x+5)}} & =\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{8}{1}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{8} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
